<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=lithography">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Communication and computing</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lithography</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1796">1796</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lithography is a planographic method of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> based on the immiscibility of oil and water: an image drawn with a greasy substance on smooth limestone or a metal plate takes the oily ink, while the wetted, etched surface around it repels it. The German author and actor Alois Senefelder invented it in the Electorate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> in 1796, and it was first used mostly for musical scores and maps. Godefroy Engelmann introduced multicolour printing, chromolithography, in 1837, and offset lithography, in which a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> cylinder carries the ink from plate to paper, has printed most books and magazines since the 1960s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lithography.jpg" alt="Lithography" width="330" height="475" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pierre Puvis de Chavannes. Imprimerie Lemercier, 57 Rue de Seine, Paris. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Centenaire_de_la_lithographie_par_Puvis_de_Chavannes_1895.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The name comes from the Ancient Greek words for stone and for writing. Traditionally the image was drawn with a greasy substance such as oil, fat or wax on a smooth, flat limestone plate. The stone was then treated with a mixture of weak acid and gum arabic, which made the parts not protected by grease more attracted to water. For <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a>, the stone was moistened; the water stayed only on the etched areas, making them more oil-repellent. An oil-based ink applied next stuck only to the original drawing and was transferred to a blank sheet of paper in a press. Unlike intaglio printing, it uses a flat plate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Senefelder had experimented with multicolour lithography in the early 19th century, and in his book of 1819 he predicted that the process would eventually be perfected to reproduce paintings. Godefroy Engelmann's chromolithography of 1837 used a separate stone for each colour, and each sheet went through the press once for every stone. The difficulty was keeping the images in register. The method suited large areas of flat colour and produced the characteristic poster designs of its period.</p><p>Lithography, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> from soft stone, largely took the place of engraving in the production of English commercial maps after about 1852. It was quick and cheap, and had been used to print British army maps during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peninsular-war">Peninsular War</a>. In modern commercial work the image is made on a flexible metal or plastic plate by a photographic process, and in offset lithography a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> cylinder carries the ink from the plate to the paper. This keeps the paper dry and allows fully automated high-speed printing.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In the early 19th century lithography had only a limited effect on printmaking, because technical difficulties remained. Engelmann, who moved his press from Mulhouse to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1816, largely resolved them, and in the 1820s artists such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eugene-delacroix">Delacroix</a> and Géricault adopted the medium. Goya made his last series of prints, The Bulls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a>, in 1828, and Daumier's lithographs were published in newspapers. Jules Chéret, called the father of the modern poster, made colour lithography a success in the 1890s, and he inspired Toulouse-Lautrec. In 1960 June Wayne founded the Tamarind Lithography Workshop in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/los-angeles">Los Angeles</a> to train master printers.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lithography” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
